---
import CtaBlock from "../../components/CtaBlock.astro";
import RelatedLinks from "../../components/RelatedLinks.astro";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { buildMeta, blogPostingSchema, organizationSchema } from "../../lib/seo";
import { formatDate } from "../../data/blog";

const slug = "why-i-bring-an-ipad-on-every-flight";
const title = "Why I Bring an iPad on Every Flight (Even Though I Limit Screen Time)";
const description =
  "A parent explains why an iPad can still belong in a toddler travel bag when screen time is limited, and which calm activities feel different on flights.";
const date = "2026-07-30";
const author = "Olga Vorona";

const meta = buildMeta({
  title,
  description,
  pathname: `/blog/${slug}/`,
  type: "article"
});
---

<BaseLayout
  {...meta}
  schemas={[
    organizationSchema(),
    blogPostingSchema({
      title,
      description,
      datePublished: date,
      pathname: `/blog/${slug}/`,
      authorName: author
    })
  ]}
>
  <article>
    <header class="page-header article-header">
      <div class="shell narrow">
        <a href="/blog/" class="back-link eyebrow">← Blog</a>
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <div class="byline">
          <span class="author">{author}</span>
          <span class="byline-sep" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
          <time datetime={date}>{formatDate(date)}</time>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="content-block">
      <div class="shell narrow prose article-body">
        <p>
          I spend a lot of energy trying to keep Leo away from over-stimulating
          screens, so it probably sounds strange that I never board a plane
          without an iPad in my bag. But the contradiction only exists if you
          assume all screen time is the same, and after years of watching my own
          son on flights, I'm pretty convinced it isn't.
        </p>

        <p>
          The first reason is simply practical. When you travel with a
          three-year-old, you are already carrying a car seat or a stroller,
          snacks for delays, spare clothes for the inevitable spill, and your own
          bag on top of it. There is no room left for the toy bin, the stack of
          picture books, the coloring set, and the activity workbook you'd
          normally spread across the living room floor. An iPad replaces all of
          that in something the size of a paperback, and it can become any one
          of those things depending on what you open. That compactness alone is
          why it earns its spot in my carry-on before almost anything else.
        </p>

        <p>
          But the real reason I trust it comes down to what he actually does on
          it, because not everything on that screen behaves the same way in his
          little nervous system.
        </p>

        <p>
          Videos and most games are the ones I watch closely, because they're
          the ones that get him overstimulated fastest. I don't know exactly how
          your child reacts, but mine has a pretty consistent pattern: somewhere
          around thirty minutes into a video, or often much sooner with a game,
          he starts to get wired in that unmistakable way, quicker to snap,
          harder to redirect, and almost guaranteed to melt down the moment I
          try to take the device away. That's the exact dynamic I'm trying to
          avoid, and it's also exactly what most "kids" apps and channels are
          built to produce, since a kid who's a little overstimulated is a kid
          who keeps watching.
        </p>

        <p>
          What surprised me is how differently he responds to a handful of other
          activities on the same device, ones that somehow don't hijack that same
          dopamine loop.
        </p>

        <p>
          Drawing is the one we use most. Almost any simple drawing or sketching
          app works, and he can sit with it quietly for a long stretch without
          the frantic energy that videos bring on. There's no score, no timer,
          no reward popping up to pull him back in, just a blank canvas and his
          own hand moving across it.
        </p>

        <p>
          A guided maze or puzzle app sits in that same quiet category, which is
          actually why I built <a href="/dennys-maze/">Denny's Maze</a> in the
          first place. He works through a maze at his own pace, there's no music
          pushing him along and no celebration screen begging him to start
          another one, and when he's done, he's just done. No fight, no "one
          more."
        </p>

        <p>
          The camera app has become a favorite too, in a way I didn't expect.
          Handing him the iPad and letting him take photos of his own feet, the
          seatback in front of him, or my tired face gives him a sense of control
          that a passive video never does, and it tires him out in the good way
          rather than the wired way.
        </p>

        <p>
          Music does something similar, just in a different direction. We use a
          music app to listen to audiobooks and stories, which keeps him settled
          without a single flashing thing on screen, and on longer flights we
          sometimes open something like GarageBand so he can bang around on a
          virtual instrument and just experiment with sound. There's no win
          condition in either case, so there's nothing pulling him to keep going
          once he's ready to stop.
        </p>

        <p>
          Reading and comic-style apps round it out. They ask for the same slow
          attention a paper book would, just in a format that fits in the bag
          along with everything else.
        </p>

        <p>
          None of this is about screen time being good or bad in some general,
          one-size-fits-all way. It's about the fact that a half hour of drawing
          and a half hour of an algorithm-fed video game are not the same half
          hour for a three-year-old's nervous system, even though they both
          technically count as "iPad time." Once I started paying attention to
          which activities left him calm and which ones left him wired, the
          packing list got a lot simpler, and so did the flight.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you're curious how I actually score apps on sounds, animation,
          rewards, and brightness, I put together a full
          <a href="/best-low-stimulation-apps-for-toddlers/">comparison of
          low-stimulation apps for toddlers</a> that walks through it in more
          detail. These are the same criteria I use before I let anything new
          onto his iPad.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>

  <RelatedLinks
    title="Related pages"
    links={[
      {
        href: "/dennys-maze/",
        title: "Denny's Maze",
        description:
          "A quiet maze app for young kids, built for flights, waiting rooms, and other in-between moments."
      },
      {
        href: "/best-low-stimulation-apps-for-toddlers/",
        title: "Low-stimulation app rankings",
        description:
          "A scored comparison of toddler apps by sounds, animations, rewards, brightness, and travel use."
      }
    ]}
  />

  <CtaBlock text="Denny's Maze is the calm maze app I built after doing this same screen-time math with my own son." />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .article-header {
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .back-link {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--muted);
    transition: color 160ms ease;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .byline {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .byline-sep {
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  .article-body {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .article-body p {
    margin-bottom: 1.6em;
  }

  .article-body p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
</style>
